# The howdy command

`howdy` is the command line for Howdy. It creates new projects, adds code to
the project you are working in, and runs the development server. It is a
single program written in Go, and
[Getting started](/docs/guides/getting-started) shows how to install it.

## Commands

- `howdy new <project_name>` creates a project. See
  [Creating a project](/docs/cli/new).
- `howdy create controller`, `howdy create pages`, `howdy create repo` and
  `howdy create migration` add a database table to the current project, with
  as much code around it as you ask for, from a JSON API to a full set of
  pages to list, add, edit and delete records. See
  [Generating code](/docs/cli/create).
- `howdy dev` runs the current project with hot reload.

Every command explains itself with `--help`, including its arguments, flags
and examples:

```sh
howdy --help
howdy new --help
howdy create controller --help
```

`howdy --version` prints the version you have installed.

## What it needs

The CLI writes Gleam code and then hands over to Gleam's own tools, so it
looks for `gleam` on your `PATH` before it writes anything, and stops with an
error if it is missing. It warns you if `erl` is missing too, because the
project will not run without Erlang.

Howdy, Howdy UI and Howdy Dev are downloaded from GitHub as Git
dependencies, so `howdy new` also needs *Git* installed. You do not need a
copy of Howdy on your machine.

## Running the project

```sh
howdy dev
```

`howdy dev` finds the project you are in and runs `gleam dev` there, which
rebuilds and refreshes the browser as you change the code. It looks for the
nearest directory with a `gleam.toml`, starting where you are and walking up,
so it works from any folder inside the project. Output is streamed to your
terminal, and Ctrl-C stops the server.

Outside a Gleam project it stops with an error rather than guessing.

## Progress output

`howdy new` and the `howdy create` commands animate their progress when they
run in a terminal. Pass `--plain` to print one line per step instead, which
reads better in CI logs and scripts:

```sh
howdy new my_app --plain
```
